//
// What runs, and which runner is asking (loft#1010, @F89).
//
// THREE runners, and they do not use one rule:
//
//   `loft --tests` / `loft test` (src/test_runner.rs), and the native entry-point
//   generator (src/main.rs) which is kept in step with it deliberately: a file that
//   names any `test_*` has said which functions are tests, so those are the whole set.
//   A file that names none keeps arity — every zero-parameter function runs, `main`
//   and value-returning ones included, and a parameter is the only way out.
//
//   The wrap harness that runs THIS file: `main` if there is one, otherwise every
//   zero-parameter function that returns Void, with no name filter at all
//   (`entry_point_names`, the P147 return filter).
//
// This file has no `main` and does name a `test_*`, so the two runners disagree about
// it on purpose: `--tests` reports `(1 fn: test_the_ordinary_shape)`, and the wrap
// harness runs that one AND `records_that_it_ran`.
//
// The counted total is the half a loft file cannot check about itself. An earlier
// version of this header said so and predicted its own failure: "if the runner's rule
// ever becomes name-based, this file keeps passing". It did, and it did. The count now
// lives in `tests/test_discovery_rule.rs`, which asserts both cases and that the native
// generator picks the same set.
//
// `not_a_test_by_arity` is a NEGATIVE control: its assertion is deliberately false, so the
// file goes red by name if arity stops being a filter.  `returns_something` cannot be one,
// because the four runners disagree about whether it is an entry point at all — see its
// comment.

// @FTR-056 — which functions a run picks up: the `test_*` a file names are the whole set, and where a file names none, a parameter is what opts out
fn test_the_ordinary_shape() {
  assert(1 + 1 == 2, "a test named test_* runs, which is the undisputed half");
}

// No `test_` prefix, no parameters, returns nothing — an entry point under the WRAP harness,
// which applies no name filter, and not one under `--tests`, which sees the `test_*` above.
// A helper that asserts a precondition is an ordinary thing to write, and which runner is
// asking decides whether it becomes a test.
fn records_that_it_ran() {
  // Nothing to compare against a previous run — each entry point gets a fresh program — so
  // this asserts only that it can execute, which is what being CALLED means.
  n = 0;
  for i in 1..4 { n += i; }
  assert(n == 6, "a zero-parameter function with no test_ prefix runs too, got {n}");
}

// Takes a parameter, so it is NOT an entry point.  The assertion is deliberately false: if
// arity ever stops being the filter, this file goes red and says so by name.
fn not_a_test_by_arity(unused: integer) {
  assert(unused == unused + 1, "loft#1010: a function WITH a parameter must not be run");
}

// Returns a value and takes none, and the four runners do NOT agree about it:
//
//   `--tests` and the shipped native generator skip it, because this file names a `test_*`.
//   `tests/wrap.rs` skips it, because its P147 filter excludes value-returning entry points
//   — calling one discards the store it answers, which leaks.
//   `tests/native.rs` RUNS it: the native corpus harness has neither filter.
//
// So its assertion is executed on the native pass of the corpus and on no other, which is
// why it is written to hold rather than to fail (loft#1293 — 165 such functions across 66
// main-less corpus files run on one half of the differential only).
fn returns_something() -> integer {
  v = 21 * 2;
  assert(v == 42, "a value-returning zero-parameter function is an entry point for the NATIVE corpus harness alone, got {v}");
  v
}
